  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nLargely dry weather is still forecast to linger through Saturday\nas large-scale surface high pressure persists. Temperatures\ntoday and Thursday will run some 10 to 15 degrees above normal.\nA mid-level shortwave passage this afternoon is only providing\nincreased cloud cover.\n\nCome Thursday night/Friday morning, a weak cold front is still\nprogged to drop across the region. As this event has come into\nthe range of the CAMs, precipitation potential north of\nPittsburgh has increased slightly as moisture from Lake Erie\nbecomes involved. Still, any rainfall is most likely to remain\nunder a tenth of an inch.\n\nRainfall potential later Sunday into Monday continues to\ndecrease, as models are now trending weaker with an Ohio Valley\ndisturbance tracking south of the main 500mb ridge axis. A\nsystem that appeared to be capable of producing a soaking\nrainfall a few days ago, now has a 30 percent or less chance of\nproducing 0.10\" in our area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsembles continue to converge on the idea of a strong closed\n500mb low over the central CONUS by 12Z Tuesday. After that\npoint, the low ejects northeastward towards the Great Lakes, and\nthere is disagreement regarding the strength and timing of the\nresulting shortwave. However, there is still a notable chance of\na wetting rainfall. Ensemble probabilities for an inch or more\nof rain are still in the 20-30 percent range for the LREF suite,\nwhile NBM probabilities are a bit higher. No flooding threats\nare anticipated given the recent dry stretch, but such amounts\ncould mitigate any building fire weather concerns.",
